## Why This Is Asked
This question probes ability to implement a branchless, constant-time parity on 128-bit data, a common requirement in cryptography and error-detection code. It tests knowledge of SWAR reductions and handling of multi-word integers.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR parity reduction
- 128-bit representation via two 64-bit halves
- Constant-time bit-twiddling without branches
- Correctness over both halves

## Code Example
```javascript
unsigned parity128(uint128 x){
  uint64_t hi = x.hi, lo = x.lo;
  hi ^= hi >> 32; hi ^= hi >> 16; hi ^= hi >> 8; hi ^= hi >> 4; hi ^= hi >> 2; hi ^= hi >> 1;
  lo ^= lo >> 32; lo ^= lo >> 16; lo ^= lo >> 8; lo ^= lo >> 4; lo ^= lo >> 2; lo ^= lo >> 1;
  return (unsigned)((hi ^ lo) & 1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt parity128 to count parity modulo 4?
- What are performance implications on 64-bit vs 128-bit architectures?

## Why This Is Asked
Rotations are fundamental in cryptography (e.g., SHA-2), hashing, and bitboard manipulation. Implementing them correctly on composite 128-bit types tests understanding of cross-word boundaries and careful handling of edge cases.

## Key Concepts
- Decompose 128-bit value into hi/lo 64-bit halves
- Handle k near 64 where rotation crosses half boundaries
- No loops required—just careful bitwise arithmetic
- Return early for k==0 to avoid undefined behavior with 64-bit shifts

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 rotl128(uint128 x, unsigned k) {
    k &= 127;
    if (k == 0) return x;

    if (k < 64) {
        uint64_t hi = (x.hi << k) | (x.lo >> (64 - k));
        uint64_t lo = (x.lo << k) | (x.hi >> (64 - k));
        return (uint128){hi, lo};
    } else {
        unsigned k2 = k - 64;
        uint64_t hi = (x.lo << k2) | (x.hi >> (64 - k2));
        uint64_t lo = (x.hi << k2) | (x.lo >> (64 - k2));
        return (uint128){hi, lo};
    }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to apply known bit-hack patterns to solve a common problem without loops or libs. Shows understanding of data-parallel aggregation and portability across compilers.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register) counting
- Bit masks and shifts to sum bits in chunks
- Portability across 32-bit integers

## Code Example

```c
#include <stdint.h>
uint32_t countBits32(uint32_t x) {
  x = (x & 0x55555555) + ((x >> 1) & 0x55555555);
  x = (x & 0x33333333) + ((x >> 2) & 0x33333333);
  x = (x & 0x0F0F0F0F) + ((x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F);
  x = (x & 0x00FF00FF) + ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF);
  x = (x & 0x0000FFFF) + ((x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF);
  return x;
}
```

